)(k−1)+2 k vertices when every pair is joined by an edge. For every $r,k\\ge3$r,k≥3, we construct a coloring at this very threshold in which every such matching contains a nested pair. Thus the nonnested threshold is larger by at least one vertex. Already with three colors, ten vertices force three disjoint edges of one color, while our coloring makes every such triple contain a nested pair. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPapers from 2026-09-17 - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/date/2026-09-17)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: yesterday; A counterexample to the quantum Hedetniemi conjecture ... The graph constructions, the certificates and the counterexample statements in the projective formulation are formalized in Lean~4.\n\n2026-09-17\n\nJulius A. Zeiss\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nA counterexample to the quantum Hedetniemi conjecture\n\nGodsil, Roberson, Šámal and Severini conjectured that the quantum chromatic number of the categorical product of two graphs equals the minimum of the quantum chromatic numbers of the factors. We disprove this conjecture: we construct explicit finite graphs $G,H$ with \\[ χ(G\\times H) \\leq 1538 < 1539 = \\min(χ_q(G),χ_q(H)).\\]The graphs are obtained from Zhu's counterexample to Hedetniemi's conjecture by using a base graph for which the Lovász theta number of the complement, and not only the fractional chromatic number, is large. The lower bound for the first factor is the theta bound. For the second factor we adapt Zhu's argument to projections that do not commute: the step that fixes the colors of a clique is replaced by identities between operators. Both lower bounds hold for colorings by projections in an arbitrary nonzero unital $C^*$-algebra. Hence the conjecture also fails for the spatial, approximate, commuting-operator and $C^*$-algebraic variants of the quantum chromatic number. We also give smaller counterexamples certified by exact integer data. The graph constructions, the certificates and the counterexample statements in the projective formulation are formalized in Lean~4.\n\n2026-09-17\n\nLorenzo Ciardo, Iris Hebbeker, Gideo Joubert, Jana Kreiß, Antoine Mottet\n\n# • INFO • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\nSchrijver-Delsarte rigidity in association schemes and undecidability of quantum graph homomorphism\n\nWe prove RE-completeness of the quantum homomorphism problem parameterised by families of graphs derived from the classic metric association schemes. These include Kneser graphs, $q$-Kneser graphs, and the complements of Johnson, Grassmann, and Hamming graphs. Our proof develops a spectral method for establishing non-contextuality of quantum polymorphisms. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Computing in permutation groups without memory (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/258082610_Computing_in_permutation_groups_without_memory)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 13.0 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; The memoryless computation model can be fully expressed in terms of transformation semigroups, or in the case of bijective functions, permutation groups. In this paper, we consider how efficiently permutations can be computed without memory. ... A program computing f∈Sym(A n) is a sequence g(1),...,g(L)∈ I such that\nArticle PDF Available\n\n# Computing in permutation groups without memory\n\n  * October 2013\n\n  * Source\n  * arXiv\n\nAuthors:\n\nPeter J. Cameron\n\n  * University of St Andrews\n\nBen Fairbairn\n\n  * Birkbeck, University of London\n\nMaximilien Gadouleau\n\n  * Durham University\n\nImage\n\nDownload full-text PDFRead full-text\n\n## Abstract\n\nMemoryless computation is a new technique to compute any function of a set of registers by updating one register at a time while using no memory. It aims at emulating how computations are performed in modern cores, since they typically involve updates of single registers. The memoryless computation model can be fully expressed in terms of transformation semigroups, or in the case of bijective functions, permutation groups. In this paper, we consider how efficiently permutations can be computed without memory. We determine the minimum number of basic updates required to compute any permutation, or any even permutation. \nComputing in permutation groups without memory\n\nPeter J. Cameron∗\n\n, Ben Fairbairn†and Maximilien Gadouleau‡\n\nOctober 23, 2013\n\nAbstract\n\nMemoryless computation is a new technique to compute any function of a set of regis-\n\nters by updating one register at a time while using no memory. Its aim is to emulate how\n\ncomputations are performed in modern cores, since they typically involve updates of single\n\nregisters. The memoryless computation model can be fully expressed in terms of transfor-\n\nmation semigroups, or in the case of bijective functions, permutation groups. In this paper,\n\nwe consider how eﬃciently permutations can be computed without memory. We determine\n\nthe minimum number of basic updates required to compute any permutation, or any even\n\npermutation. The small number of required instructions shows that very small instruction\n\nsets could be encoded on cores to perform memoryless computation. We then start look-\n\ning at a possible compromise between the size of the instruction set and the length of the\n\nresulting programs. We consider updates only involving a limited number of registers. In\n\nparticular, we show that binary instructions are not enough to compute all permutations\n\nwithout memory when the alphabet size is even. These results, though expressed as prop-\n\nerties of special generating sets of the symmetric or alternating groups, provide guidelines\n\non the implementation of memoryless computation.\n\nAMS Subject classiﬁcation: 20B30 (primary), 68Q10, 20B05, 20F05 (secondary)\n\n1 Introduction\n\n1.1 Memoryless computation\n\nTypically, swapping the contents of two variables x and y requires a buﬀer t, and proceeds as\n\nfollows (using pseudo-code):\n\nt←x\n\nx←y\n\ny←t.\n\nHowever, a famous programming trick consists in using XOR (when x and y are sequences of\n\nbits), which we view in general as addition over a vector space:\n\nx←x+y\n\ny←x−y\n\nx←x−y.\n\n∗School of Mathematical Sciences, Queen Mary, University of London, Mile End Road, London E1 4NS, UK\n\nand School of Mathematics and Statistics, University of St Andrews, Mathematical Institute, North Haugh, St\n\nAndrews, Fife KY16 9SS, UK. email: p.j.cameron@qmul.ac.uk, p jc@mcs.st-andrews.ac.uk\n\n†Department of Economics, Mathematics and Statistics, Birkbeck, University of London, Malet Street, London\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCommutative Algebra · Papers · Peer Revenue (https://peerrevenue.com/papers/math.AC?before=2026-10-29_2610.39630)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; This weighted measure of sequence length links zero-sum theory with half-factoriality, and determining its maximal value for minimal zero-sum sequences is a longstanding extremal problem. We also obtain an exact formula for the maximal cross number of minimal zero-sum sequences over subsets whose minimum distance is divisible by the rank of the ambient group minus one.\nWe resolve this problem for all homocyclic groups $C_n^r$: if $G$ is a finite abelian group with $\\mathsf D(G)\\ge4$ and $\\mathcal L(G)=\\mathcal L(C_n^r)$, then $G\\cong C_n^r$. The cross number of a sequence is the sum of the reciprocals of the orders of its terms. This weighted measure of sequence length links zero-sum theory with half-factoriality, and determining its maximal value for minimal zero-sum sequences is a longstanding extremal problem. We also obtain an exact formula for the maximal cross number of minimal zero-sum sequences over subsets whose minimum distance is divisible by the rank of the ambient group minus one. For $G\\cong C_{n_1}\\oplus\\cdots\\oplus C_{n_r}$ with $1<n_1\\mid\\cdots\\mid n_r$ and $r\\ge\\max\\{2,n_r-2\\}$, we prove \\[ \\mathsf K(G,r-1)=1+(r-1)\\sum_{p\\mid n_1} \\left(1-p^{-\\mathsf v_p(n_1)}\\right), \\] which is a key ingredient in resolving the Characterization Problem for homocyclic groups.\n\nRead the full abstract ▾\n\n2610.34288 · 25 Oct\n\nAbstract ↗ PDF ↗\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nOpenAI Mathematics — Searchable Preprint Catalogue (https://sites.pitt.edu/~hajlasz/OpenAI_Math_Catalogue.html)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3 weeks ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; The same construction disproves the Stable Coordinate Conjecture in ambient dimension five and yields smooth 𝔸^{3}-fibrations over 𝔸^{1} and 𝔸^{2} that are not Zariski-locally trivial. ... This gives a counterexample to the stable coordinate conjecture in four variables.\nThis disproves the Lipman–Zariski conjecture in characteristic zero.\n\nResult family 048: A characteristic-zero counterexample to Lipman–Zariski\n\nNo Lean formalization is listed for this manuscript.\n\nNo entry for this PDF was found in the main-result catalogue or the manuscript links in the Lean scope notes, as of 12 October 2026. This means “not listed”, rather than proof that no formalization exists.\n\nFamily 049·14 pages\n\nLean: main result formalized ↗\n\n### A stable coordinate that is not a coordinate in four variables\n\npolynomial automorphismsaffine algebraic geometry\n\nRead PDF ↗GitHub source ↗\n\nAbstract & Lean details\n\nRepository abstract\n\nWe construct an explicit degree-five polynomial over $\\mathbf C$ that is not a coordinate in four variables but becomes one after adjoining a single variable. This gives a counterexample to the stable coordinate conjecture in four variables. Every fiber is isomorphic to affine three-space, yet its embedding in affine four-space is not rectifiable. Thus the example also disproves the Abhyankar–Sathaye embedding conjecture in ambient dimension four.\n\nResult family 049: A stable-coordinate counterexample in four variables\n\nThe main result is listed as formalized in Lean.\n\nThis manuscript appears in the repository’s formalization catalogue. The scope below identifies the covered statements and limitations.\n\nThe stable coordinate conjecture predicts that a polynomial that becomes a coordinate after adjoining variables was already a coordinate. The formalization gives an explicit degree-five polynomial in four complex variables that becomes a coordinate after adjoining one variable but is not a coordinate in four variables. Every fiber is isomorphic to affine three-space, yet no fiber can be carried to a coordinate hyperplane by an ambient polynomial automorphism.\n\nThe Abhyankar–Sathaye conjecture predicts that a polynomial defining an affine-space quotient must be an ambient coordinate. For every $n\\ge4$, the formalized counterexample gives $F\\in\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]$ with quotient $\\mathbb C[x_1,\\ldots,x_n]/(F)\\cong\\mathbb C[y_1,\\ldots,y_{n-1}]$, although $F$ is not a coordinate.\n\nA companion gives $n-1$ commuting, locally nilpotent derivations, linearly independent over the polynomial ring, whose common kernel is generated by the construction's explicit polynomial and contains no ambient coordinate. The extra $n-4$ derivations are ordinary partial derivatives in the added variables. The three-variable case is not covered.\n\nRead this scope note on GitHub ↗\n\nAbhyankarSathaye.leanCommutingDerivations.leanStableCoordinateFour.lean\n\nFamily 049·6 pages\n\nLean: main result formalized ↗\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAEI-2008-056 (https://pure.mpg.de/pubman/item/item_153839_2/component/file_153838/0807.4722v2.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; general and was used also for proving Sen’s conjecture using Schnabl’s solution, ...